    answer: 'A traveller may shorten the four-rak\'ah prayers (Dhuhr, Asr and Isha) to two rak\'ahs. Fajr (2) and Maghrib (3) stay as they are. This is a mercy from Allah: "there is no blame upon you for shortening the prayer" (Qur\'an 4:101). Umar asked the Prophet ﷺ about it, and he said it is a charity from Allah, so accept His charity (Sahih Muslim 686).\n\nWhen it applies: on a journey of about 80 km (roughly 50 miles) or more, by the view of most scholars. Some scholars say there is no fixed distance. You begin shortening when you leave your town. How long you can keep shortening in one place differs: the Shafi\'i, Maliki and Hanbali schools say up to about four days of staying; the Hanafi school says up to fifteen days.\n\nCombining: the Shafi\'i, Maliki and Hanbali schools allow a traveller to join Dhuhr with Asr, and Maghrib with Isha, at the time of either. The Prophet ﷺ did this, delaying Dhuhr to Asr when he set out before noon (Sahih al-Bukhari 1111). The Hanafi school allows joining only at Arafah and Muzdalifah during Hajj. You may not skip a prayer or push it out of its day.\n\nOn a plane or train: face the qibla as far as you can and pray seated if you cannot stand. If the time will run out, pray in your seat; do not leave a prayer to be made up unless you have to.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 4:101; Sahih Muslim 686; Sahih al-Bukhari 1111.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-travel]',
