    answer: 'Praying in socks or shoes is valid as long as they are clean (free of najasa). Anas was asked whether the Prophet ﷺ prayed with his shoes on, and said yes (Sahih al-Bukhari 386). Many masjids keep carpets clean, so shoes are taken off there out of respect; follow the local custom for the place you are in.\n\nWiping over socks instead of washing the feet in wudu (masah): you may wipe over leather socks (khuffs) if you put them on after you had a complete wudu. The time limit is one day and night for a resident and three days and nights for a traveller (Sahih Muslim 276).\n\nOrdinary cotton or wool socks: the schools differ. The Hanbali school accepts any covering thick enough to walk in; the Hanafi school accepts thick socks that stay up by themselves; the Shafi\'i school requires leather or waterproof socks. Many scholars today accept thick socks. If your socks are thin, wash your feet. The schools also differ on when the period starts counting (from your first wudu-break after putting them on, or from your first wipe), so ask your imam if you rely on this.\n\nWiping ends when the time ends, when you take the socks off, or when a bath (ghusl) becomes due.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 386; Sahih Muslim 276.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]',
