      r'\bhow to perform salah\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:is|are) the (?:steps|actions) (?:of|in) (?:the )?prayers?\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:janazah|funeral|eid|jumuah|friday|witr|taraweeh|tahajjud|istikhara|travel\w*|sick|sitting|fajr|dhuhr|asr|maghrib|isha|women|woman|child|kid)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Here is a two-rak\'ah prayer from start to finish. Be in wudu, face the qibla, and stand where you will pray.\n1. Stand and make the intention in your heart. Say "Allahu akbar" (raise your hands to the ears or shoulders) and fold your hands over your chest or navel (the schools differ), right over left.\n2. Recite the opening du\'a (optional in some schools), say "A\'udhu billahi minash-shaytanir-rajim", then "Bismillah" and al-Fatihah, and say "Amin". Then recite another surah or a few verses, such as Surah al-Ikhlas.\n3. Say "Allahu akbar" and bow (ruku\'): back straight, hands on knees. Say "Subhana Rabbiyal-\'Azim" (Glory to my Lord, the Mighty) three times.\n4. Rise saying "Sami\'allahu liman hamidah" (Allah hears whoever praises Him), then "Rabbana wa lakal-hamd" (Our Lord, to You is the praise). Stand straight.\n5. Say "Allahu akbar" and go down to prostration (sujud): forehead, nose, both palms, both knees and the toes of both feet on the ground. Say "Subhana Rabbiyal-A\'la" (Glory to my Lord, the Most High) three times.\n6. Say "Allahu akbar", sit up briefly, then prostrate a second time with the same words.\n7. Stand up for the second rak\'ah and repeat from step 2 (without the opening du\'a).\n8. After the second sujud of the last rak\'ah, sit and say the tashahhud, then the blessings on the Prophet ﷺ.\n9. Give the salam, turning your head right then left: "As-salamu \'alaykum wa rahmatullah".\n\nThree- and four-rak\'ah prayers: sit for the tashahhud after the second rak\'ah, then stand for the rest and sit again at the end. Fard Fajr is 2, Dhuhr 4, Asr 4, Maghrib 3, Isha 4.\n\nThe How to Pray guide in this app shows each step in order. If you are learning, pray at your own pace and ask your imam to watch and correct you.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]',
    topic: 'how to pray',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Hand positions, the opening du\'a and the placement of the hands vary by school; this is the general outline of standard teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'touch-quran-wudu',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:touch|touching|hold|holding|carry|carrying|read|reading|recite|reciting|open|opening)\b.*\b(?:quran|mushaf)\b.*\b(?:without|with no|wudu|ablution|impure|janabah|period|menstruat\w*|unclean)\b',
      r'\b(?:quran|mushaf)\b.*\b(?:without wudu|without ablution|not in wudu|without being pure|while impure|in janabah)\b',
      r'\bwudu\b.*\b(?:touch|hold|read|recite|carry)\w*\b.*\b(?:quran|mushaf)\b',
      r'\b(?:quran|mushaf)\b.*\bwudu\b',
      r'\b(?:quran|mushaf)\b.*\b(?:phone|screen|app|tablet|digital|ebook|online)\b.*\b(?:wudu|touch|impure|bathroom|toilet)\b',
      r'\b(?:phone|screen|app|tablet|digital)\b.*\bquran\b.*\b(?:wudu|touch|bathroom|toilet)\b',
      r'\bquran\b.*\b(?:bathroom|toilet)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Touching the physical copy of the Qur\'an (the mushaf):\n- Most scholars, including the four schools, say you should be in wudu to touch or carry the written Qur\'an, based on "None touch it except the purified" (Qur\'an 56:77-79). Many explain this verse as being about the angels in the heavenly record, and use other reports to support the ruling.\n- A minority of scholars say it is not required, as there is no clear text; many modern scholars take the cautious view and say wudu is best.\n- A person in janabah (and, in most views, a woman in her period) should not touch a mushaf directly. They may hold it with a cloth, or use a translation or a phone.\n\nReciting the Qur\'an without wudu (from memory or from a screen) is allowed. A person in janabah or a menstruating woman is a school matter: the Hanafi, Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools restrict reciting; the Maliki school allows a menstruating woman to recite, and so do some other scholars, since the report quoted for the ban is graded weak (Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 131, da\'if per Darussalam).\n\nOn a phone, tablet or computer: most contemporary scholars say a screen showing the Qur\'an is not treated like a printed mushaf, so you may touch the device without wudu, but you should still treat it with respect and not take it into the toilet if you can avoid it. This is the common view, and some are stricter.\n\nWhen in doubt, make wudu first; it costs little and it is the way of respect.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 56:77-79; Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 131, da\'if per Darussalam.\n[GO:/quran]',
    topic: 'quran touching',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Touching the mushaf, janabah and screens are fiqh positions taken from standard teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'wudu-barriers',
    patterns: <String>[
