    answer: 'Ramadan is the ninth month of the Islamic calendar, the month in which the Qur\'an was revealed. Fasting (sawm) in it is one of the five pillars of Islam. Allah says: "decreed upon you is fasting as it was decreed upon those before you that you may become righteous" (Qur\'an 2:183), and "The month of Ramadhan [is that] in which was revealed the Qur\'an" (Qur\'an 2:185).\n\nWhat fasting is: from true dawn (Fajr) until sunset (Maghrib) you give up food, drink and marital relations, with the intention to obey Allah. It also means guarding the tongue and the eyes. The Prophet ﷺ said that whoever does not give up false speech and bad conduct, Allah has no need of his giving up food and drink (Sahih al-Bukhari 1903).\n\nWho: every adult Muslim who is sane and able; those who are ill, travelling, menstruating, pregnant or old are excused as explained in the exemptions. A day of fasting begins with suhoor, ends with iftar, and is filled with prayer, Qur\'an, charity and good character. The last ten nights hold Laylat al-Qadr, better than a thousand months (the night of the Qur\'an\'s descent, Qur\'an 97:1-3).\n\nThe Ramadan screen and the Islamic Calendar in this app help with the days and times. The Fasting guide goes step by step.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:183, 2:185, 97:1-3; Sahih al-Bukhari 1903.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]\n[GO:/ramadan]',
