    answer: 'Islamic law (fiqh) puts every action in one of these levels:\n- Fard (obligatory): required; reward for doing it, sin for leaving it. Examples: the five daily prayers, fasting Ramadan, zakat. A communal duty (fard kifayah) is one that is lifted from everyone once enough people do it, like the funeral prayer.\n- Wajib: in the Hanafi school, a duty just below fard (established by a text that is less certain), like witr and the Eid prayer; leaving it is sinful. The other schools treat wajib and fard as the same thing, except in Hajj.\n- Sunnah (recommended): what the Prophet ﷺ did and encouraged; rewarded, and not sinful to leave. Sunnah mu\'akkadah is a sunnah he rarely left, like the sunnah before Fajr.\n- Mustahabb / nafl (praiseworthy or voluntary): extra acts, rewarded, not emphasised.\n- Mubah (permitted): neither rewarded nor sinful.\n- Makruh (disliked): better to avoid; not sinful to do.\n- Haram (forbidden): sinful to do, rewarded to avoid for Allah\'s sake.\n\nNote that "sunnah" also means the whole way of the Prophet ﷺ (his words, actions and approvals), which is the second source after the Qur\'an.',
